  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nUpper level ridge parked across the southeast US generally remains \nin control across West Central Texas into next week. However, a \nweakness in the ridge (or something more like an inverted trough) \nbrings better low level moisture into the area for Wednesday and \nThursday. Model blends show very little in the way of precip on \nWednesday afternoon, but CAMs like the RRFS and the HRRR are much\nmore aggressive with at least isolated POPs. Given that we have \nseen some isolated showers each afternoon over the last few days, \nseeing at least showers and storms with the better moisture seems\nperfectly reasonable. Will add isolated POPs for Wednesday.\n\nThursday is a little different, as a weak surface boundary tries to \nsag south into the area. Blends pick up on this boundary acting as a \nfocus and boost POPs into the 20-30% range. Will leave this as is \nfor now.\n\nUpper ridge builds back into place after that and hot and dry \nconditions return for the weekend. Longer range forecast for next \nweek is a bigger question, with some of the longer range solutions \nshowing at least some weakening in the strength of the ridge. Not \nsure I believe this yet, given how strongly these ridges like to \nhang on. But not going to argue with the blends bringing highs down \na few degrees into the mid 90s by next Tuesday.",
